 abstract = {In the domain of non-relational databases, key-value storage engines emerge as a promising technology that is being studied widely. Recent advancements in key-value store engines have focused on leveraging the performance benefits of nonvolatile memory (NVM) and solid-state drives (SSDs). In addition, to address the limitations of a single storage device, researchers have explored strategies for aggregating multiple storage devices. Some works (e.g., SpanDB, SplitDB) proposed to put the upper levels of the log-structured merge-tree to fast storage devices (e.g., NVMe SSD), while the lower levels are stored on slower devices (e.g., SATA SSD). However, these approaches often neglect the cost-effectiveness and capacity advantages of HDDs, which remain a crucial storage medium for many applications. We seek to address this oversight by designing an optimized, scalable, and high-performance key-value store engine specifically for multi-HDD environments, multidisk key-value store (MDKV). MDKV integrates asynchronous I/O and data sharding to enhance system efficiency. MDKV utilizes asynchronous I/O to improve compaction efficiency across different levels, mitigating write stalls. Concurrently, it employs data sharding to split the logic key space into groups and store these groups in different disks, thereby reducing query latency. The experimental results demonstrate that MDKV outperforms existing techniques, showing scalability as the number of disks increases. Specifically, MDKV achieves a remarkable 66% reduction in I/O latency compared with the state of the art. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Science+Business Media, LLC, part of Springer Nature 2024.},
